{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"if test -f frontier_brief.md; then sed -n '1,240p' frontier_brief.md; fi\"","aggregated_output":"# Unverified frontier brief: bp35 level 9\n\nThis is a compact index of the latest clean WIP, not solver evidence.\nReproduce every observation you rely on with the documented local API.\nDo not reread the full proposer transcript unless a named ambiguity requires it.\n\n## Verified parent budget\n\n- Exact parent boundary: level 8 at 310 actions.\n- Remaining real-action budget under the harness cap: 290 of 600.\n- This budget is verifier evidence. If it is insufficient, optimize earlier composed legs rather than searching an uncommittable suffix.\n\n## Prior proposer progress\n\n- The comparison shows the boosted second flip does not reach the canonical second-flip state—it only recreates the canonical first-flip chamber. The difference is entirely in the lower catch tail. I’m inspecting full-versus-small shapes in those bottom cells now, because only full catches propagate; the prior coordinate tests included immutable small shapes and therefore could not stage the missing tail.\n- The shape check confirms the tail contains both propagating area-21 catches and immutable area-12 strands; coordinate-only staging remains ambiguous. A preserved probe in this workspace already claims to carry the boosted extra switch through both handoffs to the prize wall. I’m reproducing that six-choice matrix now, which is more direct evidence than further tail reconstruction.\n- The preserved boosted route also reconverges without exposing the prize, so it does not supply the missing resource. I’m changing tactics: action 7 is documented by prior same-lineage observations as stack undo. I’m reproducing exact frame restoration at the lower frontier; if verified, it enables a bounded in-place local search without the deep-clone cost that stalled the earlier BFS.\n- Action 7 restores the lower frontier exactly after clicks and movement, including terrain and avatar position. Before using it for search, I’m testing one known lethal climb followed by undo; terminal restoration is the only remaining safety condition for an in-place DFS.\n- Terminal states cannot be undone—the worker stops before restoration—so an in-place DFS would be unsafe and I’m not using it. I’m returning to compact preserved probes and indexing only their docstrings for goal-facing frontiers; this avoids broad source reads and identifies the smallest directly replayable endpoint to test next.\n- The indexed three-skip route is more promising than its name suggested: it explicitly carries a switch toward the solid prize wall without spending it. I’m reproducing its three lane variants now, with the real acceptance criterion being a goal-visible endpoint that still has a control—not merely a changed wall frame.\n- The three-skip frontier provides the missing ordering: before its final flip, the avatar can be aligned over columns 2 or 3 while the yellow trapdoors are directly below and the control is still visible. I’m removing the trapdoor under each alignment first, then flipping. This tests an actual gravity-assisted fall through the wall, unlike the earlier column-5 landing beside it.\n- This ordering works. With the avatar aligned in column 2, removing only the left yellow and then flipping enters a new deep chamber: the avatar is above a partial wall whose opening spans columns 5–7, with a full wall farther below. That is genuine new reachability. I’m walking right to the opening now; gravity should carry the avatar through as soon as it reaches an open column.\n- The trapdoor route reaches the prize chamber but is stopped above a full wall; one more reversal is required. The earlier three-skip gate can be crossed without consuming its first switch—a fact already reproduced in this attempt. I’m grafting that zero-switch crossing onto the trapdoor route. It should arrive at the full wall with one control still visible, exactly matching the remaining requirement.\n- The zero-switch gate crossing succeeds and keeps three controls, but choosing the lowest first flip leaves only one visible after column-2 alignment. The choice policy was wrong for this deeper route. I’m screening all three first switches; the accepted branch must retain at least two controls at the aligned trapdoor so one survives into the prize wall.\n- The top switch is the only viable first choice—it leaves two controls at column 2—but opening the yellow trapdoor appears to scroll both controls away before the second flip. I’m instrumenting that exact intermediate frame; if confirmed, the fix is to stage the trapdoor earlier, before the alignment-induced camera shift.\n- Opening the aligned trapdoor causes the deep transition directly; the second flip was unnecessary. The missing resource can therefore come from the four-control boost: flip once to downward gravity, move left twice onto the yellow support, remove it, and fall—no intermediate gravity turn. I’m testing that compressed route now and checking for a surviving control at the goal wall.\n\n## Preserved local probes\n\n- `frontier_scaffold.json` (2713 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_after_flip_lanes.py` (1496 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_after_wall_flip.py` (1486 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_between_barrier_flips.py` (2112 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_boost_direct.py` (1631 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_boost_finish.py` (2042 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_boost_suffix.py` (2353 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_boosted_c4_descent.py` (863 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_boosted_depth2_supports.py` (888 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_boosted_direct_descent.py` (825 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_boosted_direct_flips.py` (1166 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_boosted_direct_trapdoor.py` (1089 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_boosted_early_final_flips.py` (1078 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_boosted_fifth_supports.py` (936 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_boosted_gate_alignment.py` (1119 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_boosted_lane_climb.py` (1118 bytes)\n- `probe_l9_boosted_more_cont